A synergistically directed assembly approach to distinctive MOFs utilizing both donor-acceptor (D-A) interaction from aromatic systems and coordination interactions is presented. Based on such an approach, the coronene-tpt $\mathbf{\dot{tpt}}=2,4,6$ -tri(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine) stacks based coronene-MOF-1-4 have been successfully fabricated. Their structural discrepancies with coronene-absent control products, 1′-4′, clearly illustrate the significance of coronene-tpt based D-A interactions in these architectures. All these coronene-MOFs contain varied coronene-tpt stacks as organic secondary building blocks (SBUs), which are closely interrelated with the coordination based framework structures. Moreover, porous coronene-MOF-1 and -2 express high physicochemical stability and significant light hydrocarbons storage and separation performances.
## Background and Originality Content
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), featuring excellent crystalline, porous, and tailorable structures, serve as unique platforms for the exploration and development of new functional materials in extensive fields such as luminescence, catalysis, energy storage, gas separation, etc.[1−4] Therein, distinctive MOFs are always the pursuit of chemists for their fascinating structures and novel properties.[5] In general, secondary building units (SBUs) and linkers are the two fundamental modules for the construction of MOFs, and therefore their coordination geometries should be carefully considered for the rational design and synthesis of MOFs.[6] Due to the strong coordination binding between metallic nodes and likers, the assembly of MOFs is highly directed and commonly immune to other potential noncovalent interactions with relatively lower strength, such as hydrogen bonding and so These supramolecular interactions may serve as auxiliary forces to contribute to the construction of final structures and reinforce the stability of the whole coordination entities.[8] On the other hand, in case of assembly system featuring strong supramolecular interactions that are comparable to the oordination interaction, novel structures might be expected due to their synergistic effects. Moreover, the directed assembly of MOFs under synergistic interactions could provide new insights nto the principles and strategies for their constructions. Dincă et al. reported a novel tetrathiofulvalene (TTF) tetrabenzoate based ssp framework, MIT-25, in which three conjugated electron-rich TTF cores define an organic SBU through supramolecular π-π interactions.[9] However, the targeted introduction of π-π interactions with considerable strength could be a challenge in the aspect of ligand design, and thus has received limited investigations.
Therefore, we turn our attention to the aromatic donor-acceptor (D-A) interaction between electron-rich donors (D), commonly for polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAHs), and electron-deficient acceptors (A). The strong D-A binding between these aromatic moieties could be a promising supramolecular interaction to realize the synergistically directed assembly of MOFs with coordination interactions.[10] Fujita and co-workers have proved that the introduction of D-A interaction based on PAHs could facilitate the construction of some turreted coordination cages.[11] In addition, the abundant potential aromatic D-A systems and various arrangements make their co-driven assembly a comprehensive study. Furthermore, the highly directed stacking assignment of D-A components may bring consequently derived properties.[10c] However, in fact, MOFs containing both aromatic D-A and coordination interactions are rare. Only a few of PAHs-tpt related layered structures have been reported till now (tpt = 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine).[12] In these cases, all PAHs present uniform monomeric states and are not obviously affected by coordination patterns. The $\mathsf{P A H s^{\prime}}$ monomers behave as dividers and interact with tpt moieties. The absence of MOFs achieved by synergistically directed assembly of aromatic D-A and coordination interaction may be caused by the lack of suitable systems and efficient synthesis routs.
In this paper, we report the synergistically directed assembly strategy of aromatic stacks based MOFs by D-A and coordination interactions. With this strategy, coronene-tpt, a classic aromatic D-A system, is utilized to realize the coordination assembly of $Z n^{2+}$ and different linear dicarboxylic systems, and four products were obtained, namely $\{[\mathsf{Z n}(1,4\mathsf{-n d c})(\mathsf{t p t})_{1/3}](\mathsf{c o r o n e n e})_{1/3}(\mathsf{s o l v e n t})\}_{n}$ (coronene-MOF-1, 1,4-ndc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxy-late), $\{[\mathsf{Z n}(2\mathrm{-Br}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b d c})(\mathsf{t p t})_{1/3}](\mathsf{c o r o n e n e})_{1/3}(\mathsf{s o l v e n t})\}_{n}$ (coronene-MOF-2, 2-Br-bdc $=2$ -bromoterephthalate), {[Zn(2,6-ndc)(tpt)](coronene)}n
